Now bay is 358x610 (cy 359), frame ar 0.5869 matches. Station center is exactly bay center (194.98, 359.0). So the porthole IS centered. My visual estimate was wrong due to the reticle/caption offsets. Great.

But note bay height varies (590.5 in paused screenshot with animation? now 610). Why? In paused script, the clue sheet might have different height because `state.found` size changed? Hmm. In the geomcheck, openObject adds window to found before renderZoom, so `zoomFound` 1/4. In paused script also. Clue text same. Why bay height 610 vs 590.5? Maybe the viewport's layout changes when the transit overlay is on? Or the paused script measured after the screen was hidden? It measured bay within the page after we removed transit, but the clue sheet? Maybe because in paused script the screen was shown while `is-transit` then after removing is-transit, there's a layout change? Wait, 20px difference could be the `.footer-actions` or something. Hmm.

Actually in the paused script, at the end we removed `is-transit` and then measured, giving bay h=590.5. In geomcheck, we simply wait and measure, h=610. The difference might be the zoom screen's `.zoom-art` having margin-top:9px and the clue sheet height. Could `#clueSheet` animation (`sheetIn`) be mid-flight? No. Could the `.screen-zoom.is-active .zoom-art {animation: zoomFocus}` scale 1.035 affect getBoundingClientRect? The animation is 0.6s and should be done after 1100ms. But `is-active` class remains; animation with `both` ends at scale 1. Hmm.

Maybe in the paused script we modified timers including the 270 cleanup, but also accidentally suppressed other game timers (e.g., a 700ms one)? No. The difference could be due to screenshot time at an unusual scroll state.

Wait, in the paused script the screenshot `final-closeup` is taken after we removed is-transit; but `.screen-zoom.is-active .clue-sheet` animation may run from when is-active was added; already done. Hmm.

Look at geomcheck: viewport 390x844, bay 358x610 → top 54, bottom 664. Clue sheet top presumably ~674. In the earlier zoomrect before the reorder, bay was 358x615 (top 64, bottom 679). Now 610 (top 54, bottom 664?) The top changed to 54 because HUD top 24-45? Not sure. Anyway, the important part is ratio matching at measure time, which is verified in the ratio audit.

But wait — the ratio audit ran before the timing change but after the reorder, and reported matches for all 96. Great.

Could the bay height change after the user logs the clue and the sheet's `zoomFound` updates from "0/4" to "1/4"? We set `zoomFound` before measuring, so fine. Could the `.gun-tag` appear due to is-gun and change layout? We toggle before measure, fine. Could font loading cause text reflow after measure? Fonts are bundled and loaded via `@font-face`; by the time a user starts a case, fonts should be loaded. On first open, if fonts aren't loaded, text layout could change after measuring. This could cause a mismatch on slow first load. The app uses font-display: swap. The title/clue text uses "Special Elite". If the font loads after the close-up is rendered, the clue text height could change, resizing the bay, while the SVG viewBox was based on the old aspect. That would reintroduce cropping. Hmm. But the font files are local and likely load quickly before the user reaches the zoom screen (office → call → scene). Yet it's possible. We can guard by re-rendering the close-up on resize or after fonts load. A simple `document.fonts.ready.then(() => { if (state.screen === 'zoom') renderZoom(); })` would fix font reflow. Also a ResizeObserver on `#zoomArt` to re-render if its aspect changes significantly (e.g., orientation change / viewport resize). Currently renderZoom is called on open and on showScreen, but if the viewport resizes while zoomed, the crop won't adapt, causing cropping. For mobile, orientation changes are plausible. Adding a ResizeObserver that re-renders the close-up when the bay aspect changes could be good polish. But re-rendering while zoomed would cause a visual pop; better on resize debounce. Since the game is vertical and no-scroll, orientation changes may not be supported anyway. Still, the transition uses current bay and zoomMeta, so if the viewport changes between openObject and beginZoomIn (a single tick), no issue.

More important for alignment: `beginZoomIn` uses state.zoomMeta (measured with the closeup's final aspect) and current bay rect; zoom is derived from the ratio. If the bay aspect changed after render (e.g., fonts), the target position remains bay center and scale uses bay.width/frame.w; vertical alignment still center, but the closeup (rendered with old ratio) will be sliced. Fonts will likely be ready. Let's add `document.fonts.ready` refresh only if the current screen is zoom and no transit active, to keep the crop exact. This is cheap.

But caution: re-rendering in renderZoom after fonts load would reset `#zoomCanvas` but keep state. If the screen is zoom and the user has opened an object, re-render is fine.

Also, adding a viewport resize listener to re-render when in zoom would keep it exact. Since orientation changes may reload layout; resizing from portrait to landscape at 844x390 would make the zoom bay huge and short (ar 2.3), so the current crop would be severely sliced. A resize handler would fix it but the closeup would be re-cropped, and the label "MAGNIFIED" changes. That's okay for rotation. Let's add debounced resize handling:
```
let zoomResizeTimer;
window.addEventListener('resize', () => {
  if (state.screen !== 'zoom') return;
  clearTimeout(zoomResizeTimer);
  zoomResizeTimer = setTimeout(() => {
    if (state.screen === 'zoom' && !zoomTransit) renderZoom();
  }, 160);
});
document.fonts?.ready.then(() => { if (state.screen === 'zoom') renderZoom(); });
```
But `renderZoom` rewrites the clue text and canvas; the user might be reading. It only happens on font load once or resize, acceptable. However, re-rendering on every mobile viewport resize due to URL bar show/hide might flicker the image. Use only if aspect differs by > 1%:
```
const prev = state.zoomMeta?.ratio;
const bay = ...; if (Math.abs(bay.width/bay.height - prev) / prev > .01) renderZoom();
```
We can put that logic in a function `refreshZoomFrame()`. This is extra; maybe not needed. Keep scope.

Now, the key user-visible requirement. Let's also ensure that all close-ups use the full scene with the station in exactly the same state (e.g., clue objects added to the scene? The station drawing may vary with state? The scene render doesn't depend on found clues; all station art is static). Good.

Next, the clue "smoking gun" might be represented by an object within the station; clue text changes, not art. The close-up doesn't highlight the exact clue item (e.g., a cufflink). But the user asked object identity, not clue item. Fine.

Now, the scene screenshot at 390x844 may have been taken after the closeup rework; no effect.

Let me re-run the full flow suite, scenes, invariants after the renderZoom reorder and timing change. But before, let's improve a couple of visual things that might matter:
1. Close-up crop currently leaves the vague room context in the background at full opacity .97, then applies a 22% black overlay. For windows at the top, the crop extends into negative y where there is no room; base wall + pattern covers it, good. But the pattern uses `SCENE_WALLS[spec.wall]`; need to ensure `SCENE_WALLS` and `SCENE_FLOORS` exist and values are pattern ids. The contact sheets show appropriate wall/floor patterns, so yes.

2. The clue sheet text could push the bay to a smaller height; the crop adjusts. Verified.

3. The "MAGNIFIED · NX" label is calculated from the crop width and stage width. For porthole at 390x844 it's 4.8X. For a desk it's 2.1X. This is informative and dynamic. Good.

4. The push-in overlay animation initially shows the room at stage scale, but the target close-up is a *tall* crop. The camera zoom scale is computed to match the frame width in the bay. However, the overlay is a scene-sized image scaled; at the end, the visible bay region shows the scene at magnification 4.76, but the close-up's actual pixel display uses `bay.width/frame.w` = 358/77.73=4.605. The stage width measured ~400.9, so stage.width/400=1.002; scale=4.7557. 4.7557*1.002=4.765, vs closeup 4.605. That's a 3.5% scale mismatch! Wait: meta.zoom = (bay.width/frame.w)/(stage.width/400) = (358/77.727)/(400.93/400) = 4.6058/1.0023=4.595. But geomcheck says zoom 4.7557? Let me compute: 358/77.727 = 4.6058; stage.width maybe 388? The scene art width maybe ~388? Hmm, `stage.width` was measured when? In renderZoom, `#sceneArt` may not be laid out at 400.93 because the scene screen is not active (display none?), giving a different width. Aha! renderZoom is called after showScreen('zoom') toggles active; the scene screen becomes inactive, so `#sceneArt` has zero or stale width? getBoundingClientRect on a hidden element returns 0. If stage.width is 0, then `zoom = 400/closeup.frame.w` = 400/77.727 = 5.146. But zoom was 4.7557, so stage.width wasn't 0; maybe it's 337? Let's inspect.

This matters for the animation alignment: `beginZoomIn` uses `snapshot.art.width` from the scene when it was visible, and `scale = meta.zoom`. The scale should be `bay.width / frame.w * (400 / art.width)`? Let's derive carefully.

Overlay frame CSS width = snapshot.art.width px, containing SVG with viewBox 0 0 400 500 presumably (with preserveAspectRatio slice). The frame is then scaled by `scale`. The rendered width of a scene unit = (art.width/400) * scale px. We want the camera crop frame.w to occupy bay.width px: (frame.w) * (art.width/400) * scale = bay.width → scale = bay.width / frame.w * 400/art.width.

meta.zoom as computed: `(bay.width / closeup.frame.w) / (stage.width / 400)` = bay.width/frame.w * 400/stage.width. So if stage.width == art.width, correct. But if renderZoom measures stage.width while the scene screen is inactive (likely 0 or 0? it returned nonzero), wait — showScreen toggles is-active classes; CSS screens likely use opacity/visibility transitions rather than display:none, so getBoundingClientRect still returns dimensions. The stage width might be full even when inactive because the screen is position absolute with opacity 0. So stage.width should be ~400.9. Then meta.zoom = 4.6058 * 400/400.9 = 4.595, not 4.7557.

4.7557 = 358/77.727 / (x/400) → x/400=0.9684 → x=387.4. So stage.width must have been 387.4. The scene art width of 400.93 was from the earlier `zoomrect` at 390x844 when on the zoom screen? Wait `zoomrect` measured sceneArt while on the scene screen after goScene, giving 400.93. But at renderZoom time, the scene screen is inactive, its layout may still be constrained by the screen's padding? Hmm, if inactive screens are `visibility:hidden` with `display:flex`? The 387.4 could be the scene art width when the scene screen is inactive due to a scrollbar? Or `.scene-art` width formula uses cqh which depends on container query height of the device; the screen may be larger/smaller when inactive? Another possibility: renderZoom is called before `beginZoomIn`, while `.screen-zoom` is active; the scene screen is inactive but still has `width:min(112%,470px,calc((100cqh - 178px) * .8))`. cqh is relative to `.device` container, constant across screens. So 400.93 should be the same. Why 387.4?

Let me measure stage.width at renderZoom time. I'll add a quick evaluate reading after openObject: `state.zoomMeta.stageWidth`, `bayWidth`, and current sceneArt rect. Let's check.

